    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> established a fortified base at Dublin in 841, and the town grew into a commercial centre that stayed largely under Viking control until the Norman invasion launched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1169.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, founded in 1204, made the city the centre of Anglo-Norman and then English power in Ireland, and in the 18th century the city was rebuilt with wide Georgian streets and squares.</li><li>The Act of Union of 1800 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a> in Dublin, and after the Easter Rising of 1916, the War of Independence and the Civil War the city became the capital of the Irish Free State in 1922.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Dublin's name comes from the Irish Duiblinn, &quot;black pool&quot;, a dark tidal pool where the River Poddle entered the Liffey, on the site of the castle gardens at the rear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

