    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">841</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> establish a fortified base at Dublin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1014</span><span class="rf-text">The Irish High King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> defeats the Norse at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Battle of Clontarf</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1204</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> orders the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1592</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> founds Trinity College</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1800</span><span class="rf-text">The Act of Union ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a>, and Dublin loses its political status as a capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1916</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> takes place in the city centre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text">Dublin becomes the capital of the Irish Free State</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Humans have lived around Dublin Bay since prehistoric times. Fish traps found during the building of the Convention Centre Dublin point to habitation as far back as 6,000 years ago, and further traps near St James's Gate indicate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> activity. Ptolemy's map of Ireland, of about 140 CE, has been read as the earliest reference to a settlement near Dublin, a place he called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eblana">Eblana</a>, but doubt has since been cast on the identification, and the similarity of the names is now thought to be coincidental.</p><p>The name Dublin comes from the Middle Irish Duiblinn, "black pool", the name of a dark tidal pool where the River Poddle entered the Liffey, on the site of the castle gardens. A Christian ecclesiastical settlement known as Duibhlinn stood there before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, and the Viking settlement of about 841, Dyflin, took its name from it. Evidence from a dig in Temple Bar suggests that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> occupied Dublin before the Vikings arrived. From the 9th and 10th centuries two settlements lay where the modern city stands: the Scandinavian one on the Poddle, in the area now called Wood Quay, and the Gaelic Áth Cliath, "ford of hurdles", further up the Liffey near the present Father Mathew Bridge. Its name is still the basis of the Irish name of the city, Baile Átha Cliath. The Irish government recognised 988 as the year in which the city was settled, and Dublin celebrated its millennium in 1988.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wide-streets-commission"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wide-streets-commission.jpg" alt="Wide Streets Commission" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wide-streets-commission">Wide Streets Commission</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sackville_St_and_Gardiner%27s_Mall_in_the_1750s_by_Oliver_Grace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oliver Grace, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 841 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> established a fortified base at Dublin. The town grew into a substantial commercial centre under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-guthfrithson">Olaf Guthfrithson</a> in the mid-to-late 10th century. According to some historians, part of its early economic growth came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, which reached its height in Ireland and Dublin in the 9th and 10th centuries; Viking Dublin had a large slave market, and captives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Normandy and beyond were sold there. The Thingmote, a raised mound where the Norsemen assembled and made their laws, stood on the south side of the river beside the later castle until 1685.</p><p>After the Norman conquest Dublin prospered as a trade centre. The appointment of the first Lord Mayor in 1229 was followed by growth, and the city had a population of 8,000 by the end of the 13th century. Dublin then remained a relatively small walled medieval town. After the plague of 1649 to 1651 the city prospered again through the wool and linen trade with England, reaching a population of over 50,000 by 1700, and by 1698 the manufacture of wool employed 12,000 people. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wide-streets-commission">Wide Streets Commission</a>, established in 1757, governed the layout of streets, bridges and buildings. Guinness began brewing at St James's Gate in 1759, and linen exports rose from over 1.5 million yards in 1710 to almost 19 million yards by 1779. By 1814 the population was 175,319, and 233,159 in 1841.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada.jpg" alt="Diarmait Mac Murchada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diarmaid_Mac_Murrough_Expugnatio_Hibernica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giraldus Cambrensis, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> ruled Dublin for almost three centuries, though they were expelled in 902 and returned in 917, and the town remained largely under Viking control until 1169. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-mac-lochlainn">Muirchertach Mac Lochlainn</a> in early 1166, Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair, King of Connacht, went to Dublin and was inaugurated King of Ireland without opposition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, King of Leinster, who had been exiled by Ruaidrí, enlisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, the Earl of Pembroke, to conquer Dublin, and Strongbow declared himself King of Leinster after gaining control of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> mounted a larger invasion in 1171 and pronounced himself Lord of Ireland. Dublin became the capital of the English Lordship of Ireland, established in 1177.</p><p>The city was the heart of the Pale, a narrow strip of English settlement along the east coast. The Tudor conquest of the 16th century gave it new prominence as the centre of administration, and it was the capital of the Lordship of Ireland from 1171 to 1541 and of the Kingdom of Ireland from 1541 to 1800. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a> had met in the city from 1297. After the Act of Union Dublin was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a> from 1801 to 1922. The Irish Republic of 1919 to 1922 also had its capital there, and in 1922 Dublin became the capital of the Irish Free State, renamed Ireland in 1937.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i.jpg" alt="Elizabeth I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darnley_stage_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Dublin was a small place of around 5,000 to 10,000 people, an enclave of no more than three square kilometres hugging the south side of the Liffey. Outside the walls lay suburbs such as the Liberties, on the lands of the Archbishop of Dublin, and Irishtown, where Gaelic Irish were supposed to live after a 15th-century law expelled them from the city. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> took Dublin in 1171, many of the Norse inhabitants built their own settlement on the north side, known as Ostmantown or Oxmantown. The city was traditionally one of two languages, English and Irish. A 16th-century English administrator, William Gerard, wrote that even in Dublin most people spoke Irish, and Irish scribes and poets worked in the city through the 18th century.</p><p>Faith divided the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> founded Trinity College in 1592 as a Protestant university and ordered the Catholic St Patrick's and Christ Church cathedrals to be converted to the Protestant church. Protestants became a majority in Dublin in the 1640s, and Catholics became the majority again in the late 18th century. In the Georgian city the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wide-streets-commission">Wide Streets Commission</a> replaced narrow medieval streets with large streets, among them Sackville Street, now O'Connell Street, and five major squares were laid out. Handel's Messiah was first performed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fishamble-street">Fishamble Street</a> in 1742. The 18th-century city also had a growing slum population, and rival gangs, the Protestant Liberty Boys and the Catholic Ormonde Boys, fought street battles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-emmet"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-emmet.jpg" alt="Robert Emmet" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-emmet">Robert Emmet</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertEmmetWatercolor.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Comerford, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1315 a Scottish army under Edward the Bruce burned the city's suburbs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> reached Dublin in 1348 and killed thousands over the following decade; the plague recurred regularly until its last major outbreak in 1649. A plague from 1649 to 1651 wiped out almost half of the city's inhabitants. In the Nine Years War of the 1590s English soldiers were housed by the townsmen of Dublin and spread disease, and the English Army's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> store on Winetavern Street exploded accidentally in 1597, killing nearly 200 Dubliners. The city was besieged twice during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars">Irish Confederate Wars</a>, in 1646 and 1649, and on both occasions the attackers were driven off. In 1649 the Parliamentarian garrison routed a mixed Irish Confederate and Royalist force at the Battle of Rathmines.</p><p>After the Act of Union of 1800 the city lost the income that came with parliament and the viceregal court, and many Georgian mansions were put up for sale and became slums. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-emmet">Robert Emmet</a>'s one-day rebellion of 1803 was put down. In 1913 the Lockout, a labour dispute between the Irish Transport and General Workers Union and employers led by William Martin Murphy, left 25,000 workers on strike or locked out within a month, and rioting left three people dead. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of 1916 much of the city centre was destroyed by shell fire, and around 450 people, about half of them civilians, were killed. Fighting at the start of the Civil War in 1922 and the burning of the Custom House in 1921 destroyed more of the city's buildings. A German bombing on 31 May 1941 killed 34 civilians in the North Strand.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dublin-castle.jpg" alt="Dublin Castle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Dubhlinn_Gardens_Dublin_Castle_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J.-H. Janßen, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of Dublin's architecture and layout dates from the Georgian period, and some medieval streets around Temple Bar, Aungier Street, Capel Street and Thomas Street were less affected by the rebuilding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, Christ Church Cathedral and St Patrick's Cathedral date from the Anglo-Norman city. Trinity College holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a>, an illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> created by Irish monks around 800 CE. After the Free State rebuilt the centre, Parliament was set up in Leinster House, where it has remained.</p><p>Large parts of Georgian Dublin were demolished or substantially redeveloped in the mid-20th century, and Wood Quay, where the oldest remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> Dublin lay, was built over with the headquarters of the city's local government after a long planning struggle. Nelson's Pillar in O'Connell Street was blown up in 1966, and the Spire of Dublin was erected on the spot in 2003. From the 1980s planners worked to preserve the Georgian neighbourhoods and Temple Bar, the last part of the city with its original medieval street plan.</p><p>Dublin has a long literary history, and it produced the Nobel laureates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">William Butler Yeats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-bernard-shaw">George Bernard Shaw</a> and Samuel Beckett, and writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonathan-swift">Jonathan Swift</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde">Oscar Wilde</a>, Bram Stoker and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Ulysses</a> is set in the city. The Abbey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> was founded in 1904. In the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the city, including its suburbs, had a population of 1,263,219.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Dublin began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> fortified base of about 841 on the Liffey and became the seat of Anglo-Norman and English power in Ireland, with a castle from 1204. It was rebuilt as a Georgian city, lost its parliament in 1800, and became the capital of the Irish Free State in 1922.</em></p>

